  "answer": "Found it — the file is 1602 lines of generated `handlerN` exports, and the buggy lookup is on line 1602. Let me view the tail of the file and check how tests run.\nThe test uses `bun:test` and confirms the expectation: `shift([10, 20, 30], 0)` must be `10`. The bug is `table[key + 1]` on line 1602. Applying the minimal fix to just that lookup expression:\nFix applied. Now running the test suite:\nDone. Summary:\n\n**Bug:** In `src/registry.ts` (line 1602), `shift(table, key)` returned `table[key + 1]` — the element *after* the requested key — instead of `table[key]`.\n\n**Fix:** Changed only that lookup expression:\n```ts\n// before\nexport function shift(table: number[], key: number) { return table[key + 1] }\n// after\nexport function shift(table: number[], key: number) { return table[key] }\n```\n\n**Untouched:** All other 1601 lines (the generated `handler0`–`handler1599` exports, header comment) are preserved — the edit was a single-expression replacement on the last line only.\n\n**Test:** `bun test` → 1 pass, 0 fail (3 assertions), including the oracle checks `shift([10, 20, 30], 0) === 10`, `shift([10, 20, 30], 2) === 30`, and `handler7(1) === 8`."
